enum BankAccountType {
  CHEQUE
  SAVINGS

  /// The escape hatch for a product name no allow-list has learned yet.
  ///
  /// A statement prints the PRODUCT a client holds, not the account type the
  /// payout tuple is recorded under. Discovery prints "Transaction Account",
  /// FNB prints "EASY ACCOUNT". Each unlisted product would otherwise mean a blocked
  /// disbursement waiting on a code change, a paired migration and a
  /// deploy — `transactional` and the two FNB product prefixes were each added
  /// that way, and the treadmill never ends because banks keep naming things.
  ///
  /// OTHER means "the account-type leg of the statement match is not asserted
  /// for this client". It does NOT weaken the payout tuple: the account
  /// number, bank name and holder name are unchanged and still have to match
  /// the verified statement. Account type was never the control that stops
  /// money reaching the wrong account — the account number is.
  ///
  /// Choosing it is an authorised act. `lib/audit.ts` records who set it and
  /// why, so "why was this one waved through" has an answer at audit time.
  ///
  /// UNLIKE the retired TRANSMISSION below, both DB guards were widened in the
  /// SAME migration that added this value. That is the whole lesson of that
  /// comment: a TypeScript-only enum value clears every precondition and then
  /// fails at payout with a bare 500.
  ///
  /// TWO STEPS, in this order. Editing accountType counts as a bank-detail
  /// change, and `app/api/clients/[id]/route.ts` un-verifies every verified
  /// bank statement on a bank-detail change — correctly, because a statement
  /// verified against the old tuple is not evidence for the new one. So setting
  /// OTHER invalidates the very statement the payout gate then asks for. Staff
  /// must set OTHER first and re-verify the statement second; doing it the
  /// other way round leaves the disbursement blocked on
  /// `verified_bank_statement_missing` and reads as though the change failed.
  /// The revocation is deliberately NOT special-cased here: narrowing a control
  /// because it is inconvenient is how controls die.
  ///
  /// Barred from debit order. `lib/integrations/collections/netcash-avs-wire.ts`
  /// maps CHEQUE->"Current" and SAVINGS->"Savings"; the Netcash AVS wire has no
  /// third value, so an OTHER account cannot be represented on a debit-order
  /// mandate and the mandate route refuses it. EFT payout only.
  OTHER

  /// RETIRED — no longer selectable anywhere, do not reintroduce.
  ///
  /// It was wired through every TypeScript layer, but the DB
  /// guards were never widened: `guardLoanApplicationDebitMandateBankTupleRepair`
  /// allow-lists ('CHEQUE','SAVINGS') and `canonicalAccountType` has no
  /// transmission branch, so it returns ''. A consumer could select it, sign
  /// the mandate, clear every precondition, and then have payout fail with a
  /// bare 500 and no staff-side remedy. It was removed from the application rather
  /// than the guards being widened.
  ///
  /// The VALUE stays in the enum on purpose: dropping a Postgres enum value
  /// requires recreating the type, and this column carries live triggers. That
  /// is real outage risk for a value no row uses. It is inert
  /// — nothing can produce it and the guards reject it.
  TRANSMISSION
}

